@using Orbit.Web.Services
@inject Translations T

@* What a field is for, folded into a mark beside its label and shown when somebody asks.

   It used to be a line of small print under every field. A form of eight fields was then eight
   sentences nobody reads twice, and on a phone that is most of the screen given to explaining a form
   rather than to the form - the same complaint the folder tabs answered by folding into one button.
   The words are unchanged and none of them are gone: they are one press or one hover away instead of
   permanently in the way.

   Hover is not the only way in, deliberately. A phone has no hover, so pressing the mark opens the
   bubble and pressing it again shuts it; a keyboard reaches the mark like any other button and the
   bubble opens on focus. It is a button rather than a bare glyph with a title attribute for exactly
   that reason - a title is unreachable by touch, and appears after a delay nobody can predict.

   Two marks, one behaviour. A "?" says what a field is for, and is quiet because most readers already
   know. A "!" says something true of this screen *right now* - an entry that has an appointment behind
   it, somebody else holding the thing open, a save that is being refused - and is drawn in the warning
   colour, because that is news rather than reference. Folding those away is the same decision for the
   same reason: a form of eight fields carried three of them permanently, and the reader who needed one
   could not find it among the ones they did not. *@
<span class="field-hint-holder">
    <button type="button" class="field-hint-mark @(Warns ? "warns" : null) @(_isShown ? "on" : null)"
            aria-expanded="@(_isShown ? "true" : "false")"
            aria-label="@(Warns ? T["What to know about this"] : T["What this is for"])"
            @onclick="Toggle" @onclick:stopPropagation="true">@(Warns ? "!" : "?")</button>
    @* Always in the markup rather than drawn only while open: a screen reader reads it through the
       button's own description, and a bubble that exists only after a press is a bubble that is never
       announced. What CSS does with it is a separate question - see .field-hint-bubble. *@
    <span class="field-hint-bubble" role="tooltip">@ChildContent</span>
</span>

@code {
    /// <summary>The sentence that used to sit under the field.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public RenderFragment? ChildContent { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether this is news rather than reference - see the markup. A warning is drawn as "!" in the
    /// warning colour and is not quiet: something true of the screen right now is worth noticing, even
    /// folded away.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public bool Warns { get; set; }

    private bool _isShown;

    private void Toggle() => _isShown = !_isShown;
}
